### List users (GET)
GET https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users
Accept: application/json

### Get a user ({{user_id}} comes from examples/http-client.env.json)
GET https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/{{user_id}}
Accept: application/json

### Create a post (POST with JSON body)
POST https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "tuiter demo",
  "body": "sent from neovim",
  "userId": {{user_id}}
}

### Update a post (PUT)
PUT https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 1,
  "title": "updated title",
  "body": "updated body",
  "userId": 1
}

### Delete a post (DELETE)
DELETE https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

### 404 example — this endpoint does not exist
# A 404 means the request reached the server fine, but the URL is wrong.
# (jsonplaceholder has no /health, for example.) Not a network error.
GET https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/health

### Local test server — start it with: python3 tests/server.py
# @name local-ping
GET http://127.0.0.1:8999/api?x=1

### Response chaining — uses {{$body.*}} from the LAST response above.
# {{$body.method}} becomes "GET", {{$body.path}} becomes "/api?x=1",
# and {{$status}} is the last response code. Also try {{$timestamp}}.
# @name chain
GET http://127.0.0.1:8999/api?method={{$body.method}}&from={{$status}}

### Dynamic values
# @name dynamic
POST http://127.0.0.1:8999/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": {{$randomInt}},
  "uuid": "{{$uuid}}",
  "guid": "{{$guid}}",
  "ts": {{$timestamp}}
}
